---
description: NestJS module/layering rules — SOLID applied to controllers/services/repositories
globs: ["**/*.module.ts", "**/*.controller.ts", "**/*.service.ts", "**/*.dto.ts", "**/*.guard.ts", "**/*.interceptor.ts"]
alwaysApply: false
---

## Layering (Single Responsibility)
- Controller: routing, request/response shaping, calling one service method — no business logic, no direct Prisma calls
- Service: business logic only — no `@Req`/`@Res`, no knowledge of HTTP status codes
- Repository/Prisma access: isolate Prisma calls in a dedicated provider (e.g. `*.repository.ts` or the module's Prisma-facing service) — don't scatter `this.prisma.x` calls across multiple services for the same model

## Dependency Injection (Dependency Inversion)
- Always inject via constructor; never `new` a service/provider manually
- Depend on abstractions (interfaces) for anything that could have multiple implementations (e.g. storage provider, mail provider) so swapping MinIO/SMTP providers doesn't touch business logic

## Module boundaries (Interface Segregation)
- One module per domain (auth, mfa, tenants, billing, media, etc.) matching the existing `src/` structure
- Only export providers a module's consumers actually need from its `*.module.ts` — don't export everything by default
- Cross-module access goes through the exporting module's public service, never by reaching into another module's internals

## Open/Closed
- Extend behavior via new guards/interceptors/strategies rather than adding conditional branches to existing shared guards (e.g. don't add tenant-specific `if` checks into the global auth guard — write a composable guard)
- New MFA methods, OAuth providers, or billing providers should implement a shared interface/strategy pattern, not fork existing service logic

## DTOs & validation
- Every request body/query/param has a DTO with `class-validator` decorators
- DTOs are separate from Prisma models — never return a Prisma entity directly; map to a response DTO
- Use `class-transformer` `@Exclude()` on sensitive fields (password hashes, MFA secrets, tokens) at the DTO level, not by manual deletion in controllers

## Guards, Interceptors, Pipes
- AuthN → Guard; AuthZ (roles/permissions/tenant scoping) → Guard; response shaping/logging → Interceptor; input coercion/validation → Pipe
- Use `@Roles()` / `@Permissions()` / `@Public()` decorators consistently — don't hand-roll auth checks inside controller methods
- Any guard touching `sessionId`, `mfa_pending`, or tenant context must fail closed (deny by default) on missing/malformed data